    faq=[
        ("What are the ceremonies in Kanban?", "Kanban has no mandatory ceremonies. The Kanban Method describes seven cadences: the daily Kanban meeting, replenishment, delivery planning, service delivery review, risk review, operations review and strategy review. Teams adopt only those they need."),
        ("Are Kanban meetings mandatory?", "No. Kanban University recommends feedback loops but lets teams choose which cadences to hold and how often, and the Kanban Guide 2025.5 requires no meetings at all."),
        ("What is a cadence in Kanban?", "A regular meeting or review that provides feedback on one or more services, such as a daily meeting or a fortnightly service delivery review. The feedback must lead to action to count as a feedback loop."),
        ("How long should a daily Kanban meeting be?", "About 15 minutes is a common time-box. Kanban University sets no length; keep it short by walking the board and taking detailed problem-solving offline."),
        ("What is the difference between a Kanban stand-up and a Daily Scrum?", "A Kanban meeting reviews the flow of items against WIP limits, item age and the SLE. The Daily Scrum inspects progress toward the Sprint Goal and adapts the Sprint Backlog. Both are often run as a board walk."),
        ("Does Kanban have sprint planning?", "No. Kanban has no Sprints. Its closest equivalents are the replenishment meeting, which decides what starts next, and the delivery planning meeting, which decides what is handed over and when."),
        ("What happens in a service delivery review?", "The service owner, team and customer representatives compare delivery with expectations, using measures such as SLE attainment, cycle time percentiles, throughput, ageing items and customer feedback, then agree policy changes or actions."),
        ("Do Kanban teams hold retrospectives?", "Many do. Kanban University's Official Guide suggests a team retrospective every two weeks or monthly, focused on flow data and experiments."),
        ("What is a risk review in Kanban?", "A regular review, often monthly, of what is likely to stop the service delivering. It commonly uses blocker clustering to rank causes of delay and agrees responses such as new policies or classes of service."),
        ("Who attends a Kanban strategy review?", "Senior leaders and service owners, usually quarterly, to decide which services and types of work the organisation should focus on, informed by data from the operations and service delivery reviews."),
        ("Which Kanban meetings should a new team start with?", "A daily Kanban meeting and a replenishment meeting, ideally adapted from meetings the team already holds. Add a service delivery review once you have cycle time data, and higher-level reviews when several services interact."),
        ("How much time do Kanban meetings take?", "It depends on the calendar you choose. In the illustrative example in this guide, a team member spends about 7.5 hours a month, or 5% of working time, in a daily meeting, a fortnightly SDR and a monthly retrospective."),
    ],
